Generated runsplash cannot directly mutate aichat internals. It can only send user intent through agent.notify(event_id, payload), and the Host decides what to do through an action whitelist and payload schema. Button { text: "+1" on_click: || agent.notify("inc", {})
} TextInput { text: "{{state.input.new_item.value}}" on_change: |text| agent.notify("app.input.set", {key: "new_item", text: text})
} aichat handles the live wire as follows: sequenceDiagram participant R as runsplash callback participant W as widgets::Splash participant A as aichat App participant D as Local data/state participant UI as Visible UI R-&gt;&gt;W: agent.notify(event_id, payload) W-&gt;&gt;A: SplashAction::Notify {event_id, payload_json} A-&gt;&gt;A: match whitelisted action A-&gt;&gt;D: mutate data/state or send agent prompt A-&gt;&gt;UI: refresh data/state templates Typical aichat dispatch includes: inc, dec, and reset update the counter. timer.* updates the timer. calculator.* updates the calculator. app.input.set updates input draft state in HostState. app.collection.* updates local collection data. ask_ai wraps the current interaction as a prompt and sends it to the selected backend. The key design point is: the Template emits intent, while the Host owns state mutation. This allows LLM-generated UI to be interactive without promoting arbitrary script output to Host authority.
